WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:14.228
My name is Hilary Brown 
I work at the Wellcome Trust Sanger Institute.

00:00:14.228 --> 00:00:20.442
I'm currently studying for a PhD based on 
the Intestinal Microbiota of the Human Gut.

00:00:20.934 --> 00:00:28.098
The human intestinal microbiota is mostly made up of bacteria in addition to some viruses and fungi

00:00:28.098 --> 00:00:37.845
and these bacteria act together as a community, so that they work with each other for their own benefit but for our benefit as well.

00:00:37.845 --> 00:00:44.387
If there is an imbalance in this community and some species increase in abundance or others decrease

00:00:44.387 --> 00:00:48.912
this can have a negative effect on our health.

00:00:48.912 --> 00:00:57.187
We want to understand how this community affects our health and also identify ways to restore this balance again.

00:00:57.187 --> 00:01:07.524
The majority of the intestinal microbiota are beneficial but some of them can be harmful and this is why we study them in a CL2 lab.

00:01:07.524 --> 00:01:15.187
CL2 stands for Containment Level 2 and that means we have certain safety procedures in place to protect ourselves.

00:01:15.187 --> 00:01:19.276
If you are taking antibiotics then you're not allowed to enter the lab.

00:01:19.276 --> 00:01:26.865
This is because antibiotics - while they will kill the bacteria that are making you sick - they can also kill a lot of the good guys,

00:01:26.865 --> 00:01:31.837
which causes an imbalance in your gut and leaves you more prone to further infection.

00:01:31.837 --> 00:01:35.075
Only authorised people are allowed to work in the lab.

00:01:35.075 --> 00:01:43.001
When I enter the lab I put on a lab coat and gloves. These protect me from any spillages or bacterial contamination.

00:01:43.001 --> 00:01:49.609
Most of the bacteria that live in your gut are anaerobic; this means they cannot survive in the presence of oxygen.

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:53.828
Because of this we work with the bacteria in anaerobic hoods.

00:01:53.828 --> 00:02:03.060
These cabinets are sealed. They are constantly being pumped with anaerobic gas that contains hydrogen, carbon dioxide and nitrogen.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:08.555
This keeps all of the oxygen out and means the bacteria can survive inside them.

00:02:08.555 --> 00:02:12.611
The first thing I need to do is to enter the anaerobic cabinet.

00:02:12.611 --> 00:02:21.312
I place the sleeves over my arms and then by pressing these pedals, I can suck all of the air out of the sleeves.

00:02:21.312 --> 00:02:25.924
The other pedal then blows more anaerobic gas into the sleeves.

00:02:25.924 --> 00:02:31.419
I repeat this process three times to ensure I’ve removed as much oxygen as possible

00:02:31.419 --> 00:02:36.261
before opening the doors and entering the anaerobic cabinet.

00:02:36.261 --> 00:02:40.611
To study these bacteria we need to grow them on Agar plates.

00:02:40.611 --> 00:02:48.243
The Agar that the bacteria grow on contains lots of nutrients that would also be present in the human gut.

00:02:48.243 --> 00:02:56.421
When you streak the bacteria onto a new plate you can often see new colonies growing 24 hours later.

00:02:56.421 --> 00:03:01.622
Here I have an individual bacterial species growing on a plate.

00:03:01.622 --> 00:03:07.542
One of the jobs I regularly have to do is to 
re-streak these onto new plates.

00:03:07.542 --> 00:03:11.991
This is because the bacteria will use up all the available nutrients.

00:03:11.991 --> 00:03:16.014
When I'm working in the anaerobic hood I have to work aseptically.

00:03:16.014 --> 00:03:21.642
This means working as clean as possible and trying to prevent contamination of my experiment.

00:03:21.642 --> 00:03:28.969
For example, if I'm streaking bacteria on to a new plate, I will use an Inoculating loop but I will only use this loop once –

00:03:28.969 --> 00:03:35.511
after using it I will discard it and I will use a new loop before moving on to the next plate.

00:03:35.511 --> 00:03:41.726
There are two ways in which I study these bacteria; one is by understanding their phenotype.

00:03:41.726 --> 00:03:48.468
This involves studying their biology; how they live and grow, how they respond to different environmental conditions.

00:03:48.468 --> 00:04:00.866
The other is by studying the genotype. We extract the DNA from the bacteria and then we do whole genome sequencing of this DNA.

00:04:00.866 --> 00:04:08.945
When the sequencing is finished I then analyse the data on my computer using various bioinformatic analysis tools.

00:04:08.945 --> 00:04:14.637
Using these tools I can look at how closely related  these bacteria are to each other

00:04:14.637 --> 00:04:19.281
and I can also identify new species that no one has cultured before.

00:04:19.281 --> 00:04:27.982
Studying the intestinal microbiota is very exciting because it's moving very quickly from basic research towards therapeutics.

00:04:27.982 --> 00:04:33.085
This is unusual really for a field of biology, 
usually it takes a much longer time

00:04:33.085 --> 00:04:37.861
before we start seeing new drugs and new medicines on the market place.

